partialText:
[Cheonji Ilbo Chungbuk=Reporter Kim Heung-soon] Two years have passed since the 2023 Osong underpass flood in Cheongju, which killed 14 people, yet the truth remains unclear and no one has been held responsible.

Bereaved families say they seek not compensation but accountability. They have appealed Governor Kim Young-hwan&rsquo;s non-indictment and are demanding Mayor Lee Beom-seok be held legally accountable.

President Lee Jae-myung pledged in his inauguration speech on June 3 to investigate national disasters including the Osong tragedy, but civic groups stress that &ldquo;action matters more than words.&rdquo;

Cheongju City has updated disaster manuals and installed remote shutoff systems, but the families say &ldquo;there&rsquo;s been no real change.&rdquo;

Meanwhile, an official from North Chungcheong Province stated, &ldquo;We have been informing the bereaved families about our efforts to prevent recurrence and are working in cooperation with relevant agencies as the rainy season approaches.&rdquo;

Even two years after the tragedy, Osong remains a place of memory&mdash;still bearing the weight of unresolved truths and unaccounted responsibility.

&ldquo;Governor Kim must be punished through appeal&rdquo;

Cheongju mayor issues brief apology, claims &lsquo;no fault&rsquo;

Bereaved families: &ldquo;We want accountability and prevention&rdquo;

&nbsp;

President Lee: &ldquo;Osong tragedy must be uncovered&rdquo;

Citizens&rsquo; coalition: &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t just beat around the bush&rdquo;



<img alt="Firefighters conduct final water drainage efforts after the Miho River embankment collapse flooded the Gungpyeong 2 Underpass in Cheongju’s Heungdeok District, July 17, 2023. (Source: Newsis)" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3281622_3348703_5946.jpg" />
Firefighters conduct final water drainage efforts after the Miho River embankment collapse flooded the Gungpyeong 2 Underpass in Cheongju&rsquo;s Heungdeok District, July 17, 2023. (Source: Newsis)



[Cheonji Ilbo Chungbuk=Reporter Kim Heung-soon] Sixty thousand tons of water swallowed an underpass in just three minutes. At 8:40 a.m. on July 15, 2023, a torrential flood in the Gungpyeong 2 Underpass in Osong-eup, Cheongju-si, Chungbuk Province, claimed the lives of 14 people and injured 10 others. Among the victims were the driver and passengers of bus No. 747 and several commuters on their way to work.

Since that day, &lsquo;Osong&rsquo; has become more than just a geographical name&mdash;it is now synonymous with a national disaster. However, two years later, bereaved families and survivors still lament, &ldquo;Nothing has been resolved.&rdquo;

At a press conference in Cheongju on June 12, Choi Eun-kyung, representative of the victims&rsquo; families, said angrily, &ldquo;There has been no truth, no accountability, no apology,&rdquo; adding, &ldquo;Even the memorial altar would not have been set up if we hadn&rsquo;t demanded it.&rdquo; According to Choi, the joint memorial altar was taken down after the 49th-day rites but was later reinstated on the first floor of the Cheongju City Hall labor union building after strong demands from families.

&ldquo;We&rsquo;ve had to fight tooth and nail for everything since the day of the disaster,&rdquo; she said. &ldquo;We&rsquo;re not asking for compensation. We want those responsible to be held accountable and for society to remember this tragedy.&rdquo;

Families of the victims said they had repeatedly requested explanations, prevention measures, and meetings with responsible officials from both the city and provincial governments. They particularly criticized Chungbuk Governor Kim Young-hwan, saying, &ldquo;He promised everything&mdash;memorials, prevention measures, everything we wanted&mdash;but as time passed, his promises faded and became nothing but empty words.&rdquo;

At the same press conference, the Citizens&rsquo; Countermeasure Committee stated, &ldquo;Since the Serious Accidents Punishment Act targets the top official in charge, not only Mayor Lee Beom-seok but also Governor Kim must be held accountable in the Daejeon High Court,&rdquo; criticizing the decision not to indict the governor.

◆ &ldquo;Warnings and reports existed&mdash;but there was no control&rdquo;


<img alt="On the first anniversary of the Osong disaster (July 15, 2024), Mayor Lee Beom-seok and other Cheongju officials lay flowers at a joint memorial altar at the city’s temporary government building. (Provided by Cheongju City Hall)" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3281622_3348704_052.jpg" />
On the first anniversary of the Osong disaster (July 15, 2024), Mayor Lee Beom-seok and other Cheongju officials lay flowers at a joint memorial altar at the city&rsquo;s temporary government building. (Provided by Cheongju City Hall)



At 5:21 p.m. on July 14, the day before the disaster, the 119 emergency center received its first call: &ldquo;The embankment may collapse.&rdquo; Yet no on-site action was taken.

Later that night at 11 p.m., Chungbuk Province declared Emergency Level 1, and at 4:10 a.m. on the 15th, a flood warning was issued for the Miho River. By 6 a.m., water levels had already reached a critical level. Still, no traffic was blocked. No on-site warnings, no resident notifications, no road closures.

At 8:40 a.m., a section of the Miho embankment collapsed, sending 60,000 tons of water rushing into the underpass. Sixteen vehicles&mdash;including Bus 747 and private cars&mdash;were submerged, and many occupants failed to escape.

CCTV footage from the underpass captured it all: signs of broken windows, victims trapped by seatbelts, and the final calls made on their phones. &ldquo;We still can&rsquo;t take our eyes off the footage from that day. It&rsquo;s like time stopped,&rdquo; said the families.

Lee Seung-hoon, a bereaved family member, stated, &ldquo;There were warnings, there were reports, and there were hazard signs at the site&mdash;but no one acted. This wasn&rsquo;t a natural disaster. It was man-made.&rdquo;

◆ Mayor in court: &ldquo;The city is not responsible&rdquo;


<img alt="[Cheonji Ilbo Cheongju=Reporter Kim Heung-soon] Cheongju Mayor Lee Beom-seok, charged with causing civilian deaths under the Serious Accidents Punishment Act, speaks before entering court for his first trial on June 12. ⓒCheonji Ilbo 2025.06.16." src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3281622_3348705_414.png" />
[Cheonji Ilbo Cheongju=Reporter Kim Heung-soon] Cheongju Mayor Lee Beom-seok, charged with causing civilian deaths under the Serious Accidents Punishment Act, speaks before entering court for his first trial on June 12. ⓒCheonji Ilbo 2025.06.16.



At his first trial on June 12, Cheongju Mayor Lee Beom-seok denied all charges of violating the Serious Accidents Punishment Act. &ldquo;The city wasn&rsquo;t responsible for managing the embankment,&rdquo; he claimed. &ldquo;It was under the jurisdiction of the Multifunctional Administrative City Construction Agency (MACCA), which was conducting river construction.&rdquo;

His legal team added, &ldquo;The construction was being carried out by the Geumgang Basin Environmental Office, and by law, maintenance responsibility lies with the Minister of Environment.&rdquo;

Prosecutors countered, &ldquo;The city was the de facto manager and failed to inspect the site or allocate a proper budget, thus causing the major civilian disaster.&rdquo; This is the first case where a local government head is being tried under the Serious Accidents Punishment Act, drawing national attention.

Previously, the site manager was sentenced to six years in prison by the Supreme Court, and over 30 individuals, including officials from Kumho Construction, were indicted. Although Governor Kim was not prosecuted, bereaved families have filed an appeal, demanding reconsideration of his legal responsibility.

Rep. Lee Yeon-hee (Democratic Party, Cheongju Heungdeok) stated during last year&rsquo;s parliamentary audit, &ldquo;This investigation amounted to nothing more than &lsquo;cutting off the tail.&rsquo; We need a national investigation to uncover the full truth.&rdquo;

◆ &ldquo;Just the beginning&rdquo;&hellip; President Lee&rsquo;s pledge brings hope and doubt


<img alt="On June 12, the Victims and Survivors Association and Citizens’ Committee hold a press conference in front of Cheongju District Court, calling for the indictment of Governor Kim Young-hwan and strict punishment for Mayor Lee Beom-seok. (Source: Newsis)" src="https://www.newscj.com/news/photo/202506/3281622_3348706_456.jpg" />
On June 12, the Victims and Survivors Association and Citizens&rsquo; Committee hold a press conference in front of Cheongju District Court, calling for the indictment of Governor Kim Young-hwan and strict punishment for Mayor Lee Beom-seok. (Source: Newsis)



On June 3, President Lee Jae-myung said in his inauguration speech, &ldquo;We will thoroughly uncover the truth behind the Sewol Ferry, Itaewon, and Osong underpass disasters.&rdquo; It was the first official promise of a state-led reinvestigation. He added, &ldquo;The safety and lives of citizens are the state&rsquo;s foremost responsibility,&rdquo; and &ldquo;We will not ignore the tears of bereaved families.&rdquo;

In response, the Citizens&rsquo; Coalition emphasized, &ldquo;Actions, not words, are what matter now.&rdquo; Kim Ki-yeon, head of the Chungbuk branch of the Korean Confederation of Trade Unions, said, &ldquo;We sincerely hope the president&rsquo;s promise isn&rsquo;t just a hollow gesture.&rdquo;

A survivor, referred to as A, said, &ldquo;I still suffer nightmares from that day.&rdquo; He revealed that he&rsquo;s been undergoing psychiatric treatment and taking sleep medication, but has recently started experiencing headaches again. &ldquo;It wasn&rsquo;t just water rushing in&mdash;it was the trauma of being trapped and abandoned,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;Only by revealing the truth can I begin to heal.&rdquo;

Both the bereaved and civic groups have criticized the government&rsquo;s tendency to respond to each disaster with temporary laws and ad hoc investigative committees. A spokesperson for the Citizens&rsquo; Committee argued, &ldquo;These temporary committees disappear after their terms end, along with their findings and records. We need to enact the Basic Law on Life and Safety and create a permanent investigative body.&rdquo;

Cheongju City and Chungbuk Province have since revised parts of their disaster response manuals and installed remote shutdown systems and emergency ladders at the Gungpyeong 2 Underpass.

However, the bereaved families criticized the changes as &ldquo;mere window-dressing,&rdquo; claiming there has been no substantial change. Choi Eun-kyung remarked, &ldquo;We always have to demand and protest to get any action,&rdquo; and stressed, &ldquo;Only when the truth is uncovered and those responsible are punished will public officials start taking their duties seriously.&rdquo;

She added, &ldquo;Some people say, &lsquo;Enough with the yellow ribbons.&rsquo; But would you say that if it were your child who died?&rdquo; She concluded by appealing to the public: &ldquo;Until my mother died in this accident, I never really thought about tragedies like Sewol or Itaewon. But this isn&rsquo;t just politics&mdash;it&rsquo;s about your child, your family.&rdquo;

Survivor A echoed, &ldquo;We are all potential victims and survivors. Protecting people&rsquo;s lives and safety is the most important role of the state. Please stay alert so no more victims are created.&rdquo;

Meanwhile, a Chungbuk provincial government official said, &ldquo;We have been briefing bereaved families on our efforts and are working with relevant agencies as the monsoon season approaches.&rdquo;

Even after two years, Osong remains a place of memory&mdash;still bearing the weight of unresolved truth and unaccounted responsibility.
